(The two neighbours of #97's path. A BUFFER appended into a caller's list
 through a parameter goes down the same list-slot path a text does - it is a
 string-tagged slot - so it widens the caller's list and reads back as its
 contents, not as its address. A nested LIST element is the same shape one
 tag over. A MAP does not share the path: a map slot always carries its
 value's tag, so `Set <map>'s "<key>" to` through a parameter already read
 back as what it is; this pins that it still does. docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #97.)

To 'stash' with a list called kept and a buffer called scratch.
  append scratch to kept.

To 'nest' with a list called outer and a list called inner.
  append inner to outer.

To 'record under' with a map called ledger and a text called label.
  Set ledger's "note" to label.

(A buffer through the parameter.)
a buffer called scratch is 16 bytes in size.
Copy "buffered" to scratch.
a list called kept is [].
'stash' of kept and scratch.
Print "kept last: {kept's last}".

(A list through the parameter, into a list.)
a list called outer is [].
a list called inner is [1, 2].
'nest' of outer and inner.
Print "outer last: {outer's last}".

(A map the caller proved holds numbers, given a text through a parameter.)
a map called ledger is {"count": 3}.
'record under' of ledger and "written".
Print "note: {ledger's \"note\"}".
Print "count: {ledger's \"count\"}".
